From: BysevenOffical <38542005+Fansirsqi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:50:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] fix(linux): make window close, quit and the tray icon
 work

Closing the window did nothing and the process could only be killed with
`pkill`:

* tray_manager's Linux implementation has no setToolTip, so
  DesktopTrayManager.ensureInitialized() threw MissingPluginException and
  "minimise to tray" never worked;
* the fallback path then awaited windowManager.destroy(), which does not return
  on KDE/Wayland: the window was gone but the process stayed alive, while
  setPreventClose(true) made the window impossible to close.

Initialise the tray at startup so the icon and its Show/Quit menu are reachable
at all (upstream only initialises it on the first "minimise to tray" close),
tolerate the missing tooltip, use a bundled PNG for the AppIndicator icon
(relative .ico paths are not resolvable there), verify that hide() actually hid
the window and quit instead of pretending, and exit without awaiting window
destruction, with a timeout around proxy shutdown and a re-entry guard.

Verified on KDE Wayland + XWayland: the icon appears on startup, "minimise to
tray" hides the window and the icon restores it, "quit" exits within a second.
